      "response": "Agency/Agencies loaded: agency-steward\nAgency/Agencies delegated: none\nSkills loaded: none\nActual Model selected: none observed\nRecruited via: failed; workforce_inference_failed; staffing_critic_rejected; critic_wrong_neighbor_selection\n\nThe supplied function has two correctness defects, one of which is the core bug. This analysis is based only on the code in your message. Nothing was read from disk, and no tests were run.\n\n**Defects in the original**\n\n- **Floor division discards the fractional part.** The `//` operator truncates toward negative infinity, so `average([1.0, 2.0])` returns `1.0` instead of `1.5`. With negatives the rounding direction is also surprising, since `average([-1, -2])` gives `-2` rather than `-1.5`.\n- **Empty input raises the wrong exception.** With `[]`, `len(values)` is zero, so the call fails with `ZeroDivisionError`. That leaks an arithmetic detail instead of reporting a bad argument.\n- **Single-pass iterables are silently mishandled.** If a generator is passed, `sum` consumes it, and `len` then raises `TypeError`. This is a lesser issue, but a correct fix should not make it worse.\n\n**Corrected function**\n\n```python\ndef average(values):\n    \"\"\"Return the arithmetic mean of a nonempty sequence of numbers.\"\"\"\n    values = list(values)\n    if not values:\n        raise ValueError(\"average() requires at least one value\")\n    return sum(values) / len(values)\n```\n\nTrue division with `/` preserves fractional results. Materialising into a list makes generators work and lets the emptiness check happen before any arithmetic. The explicit check produces a clear `ValueError` instead of a `ZeroDivisionError`.\n\n**Regression tests**\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\n\ndef test_average_returns_fractional_mean():\n    assert average([1.0, 2.0]) == 1.5\n\n\ndef test_average_handles_negative_numbers():\n    assert average([-1, -2, -3]) == -2.0\n    assert average([-3, 1]) == -1.0\n\n\ndef test_average_empty_input_raises_value_error():\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError):\n        average([])\n```\n\nThe first test would fail against the original because floor division returns `1.0`. The second catches the floor-toward-negative-infinity behaviour, since the original gives `-2` for `[-3, 1]`. The third fails against the original because it raises `ZeroDivisionError` rather than `ValueError`.",
